    "message": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": "I spent 6 weeks shipping a feature our users begged for.\n\nCustom dashboards. Drag-and-drop widgets. Export to PDF. Beautiful, fast, on time.\n\nLaunch day: 🎉\n\nWeek 1: 3% adoption.\nWeek 4: basically zero.\n\nI was crushed. Then I got curious and called 15 of the users who'd requested it.\n\nHere's what I learned:\n\n**1. They didn't want dashboards. They wanted answers.**\nWhat they really meant by \"custom dashboards\" was \"tell me if we're on track on Monday morning without digging.\" We built a power tool. They needed a 30-second email digest.\n\n**2. \"Everyone wants it\" meant 3 loud voices.**\nWe'd confused feedback volume with feedback breadth. We never validated with data, prototypes, or the silence from the other 97%.\n\n**3. We shipped output, not outcome.**\nSuccess was \"launch on time,\" not \"solve the problem weekly.\" We celebrated merging PRs instead of changing behavior.\n\nNow I live by this: Fall in love with the problem, not your solution.\n\nBefore I build anything, I ask:\n→ What's the job to be done in the user's words?\n→ How will we know it's working 30 days after launch?\n→ What's the smallest version that could deliver value?\n\nThat failed feature? We replaced it with a simple Monday Slack summary. 68% weekly open rate. Took 4 days to build.\n\nSometimes the best product work is unbuilding your assumptions.\n\nWhat's a feature YOU shipped that nobody used — and what did it teach you? 👇\n\n#ProductManagement #Startups #UX #Leadership #LessonsLearned",
